     This Protection applies only at pair-level, and will never lock all pairs globally.
     This Protection does not consider `lookback_period` as it only looks at the latest trade.
 
+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` looks at the trades that closed within `lookback_period` in minutes (or in candles when using `lookback_period_candles`) for a pair, ordered from most recent to oldest, and counts how many of the most recent trades in a row lost money (`close_profit` below `required_profit`, defaulting to 0.0).
+Once `trade_limit` or more losing trades happened back-to-back with no winning trade in between, that pair is locked for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`). A single winning (or breakeven) trade resets the streak.
+
+This applies across all pairs, unless `only_per_pair` is set to true, which will then only look at one pair at a time. When evaluated bot-wide (`only_per_pair: false`), a losing streak is counted across trades for all pairs combined, so trading is halted everywhere once too many losses happen in a row, regardless of which pairs they occurred on.
+
+For futures bots, setting `only_per_side` will make the bot only consider one side, and will then only lock this one side, allowing for example shorts to continue after a series of long losses.
+
+The below example stops trading a pair for 12 candles after 4 consecutive losing trades within the last 7 days (`24 * 7` 1h candles).
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "lookback_period_candles": 168,
+            "trade_limit": 4,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 12,
+            "required_profit": 0.0,
+            "only_per_pair": False,
+            "only_per_side": False
+        }
+    ]
+```
+
 ### Full example of Protections
 
 All protections can be combined at will, also with different parameters, creating a increasing wall for under-performing pairs.
